# ant

The unified CLI for the [Autonomi](https://autonomi.com) network: store and retrieve data, and
manage local nodes.

This package distributes the same signed binary published on the
[ant-client releases page](https://github.com/WithAutonomi/ant-client/releases). It exists so
that `ant` can be installed anywhere npm reaches — including agent sandboxes and CI runners that
allow package-manager traffic but block direct binary downloads.

## Install

```sh
npm install -g @withautonomi/ant
ant --help
```

The beta channel tracks pre-release builds:

```sh
npm install -g @withautonomi/ant@beta
```

## Supported platforms

| Platform | Architecture |
|---|---|
| Linux | x86_64, ARM64 |
| macOS | x86_64, Apple silicon |
| Windows | x86_64 (also used on ARM64, under emulation) |

Linux builds are statically linked against musl, so they run on any distribution.

The binary lives in a per-platform companion package (`@withautonomi/ant-linux-x64` and friends)
declared as an optional dependency, so `npm install` downloads only the one your machine needs.
Installing with `--no-optional` or `--omit=optional` skips it and leaves `ant` unable to run.

## Configuration

`ant` joins the network through a list of bootstrap peers. It works out of the box: the same
peer list published in the release archives is compiled into the binary, and is used whenever no
config file is present.

To customise the list, put your own `bootstrap_peers.toml` here — it takes precedence over the
built-in one:

| Platform | Path |
|---|---|
| Linux | `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ant/bootstrap_peers.toml`, or `~/.config/ant/bootstrap_peers.toml` |
| macOS | `~/Library/Application Support/ant/bootstrap_peers.toml` |
| Windows | `%APPDATA%\ant\bootstrap_peers.toml` |

Installing normally writes that file for you, but npm 12 blocks package install scripts by
default, so it often will not appear. Nothing breaks when it doesn't. If you want the file so you
can edit it, either install with

```sh
npm install -g --allow-scripts=@withautonomi/ant @withautonomi/ant
```

or copy it out of the installed platform package. Individual runs can always override everything
with `ant -b <multiaddr> …`.

## Updating

```sh
npm update -g @withautonomi/ant
```

`ant update`, which self-replaces the binary for installs made from the release page, detects an
npm-managed install and tells you to use npm instead — otherwise npm's view of the installed
version would silently diverge from what is on disk, and the next `npm update` would roll you
back.

## Uninstall

```sh
npm uninstall -g @withautonomi/ant
```

Your config directory is left alone. Delete it by hand if you want it gone.

## Verifying what you installed

Packages are published from the ant-client release workflow with
[npm provenance](https://docs.npmjs.com/generating-provenance-statements), so npm records which
workflow run and commit produced each tarball. The binaries are copied verbatim from the release
archives, whose checksums and ML-DSA-65 signatures are verified during packaging, so they are
byte-identical to the corresponding GitHub release assets.

## Other install methods

The release page, `install.sh` and `install.ps1` all still work and are unaffected by this
package. See the [project README](https://github.com/WithAutonomi/ant-client#readme).

